# Cyrius — Agent Guides
# Read-this-first rules for AI agents writing or modifying Cyrius code.
# Plus tcyr/test conventions and known anti-patterns.
# Refreshed 2026-07-27 at v6.4.82 (the v6.4.x CLOSEOUT). Two rules here
# were ACTIVELY WRONG and were corrected by RUNNING the compiler, not by
# re-reading the doc: mixed `&&`/`||` parses fine (fixed v6.3.36; this
# file still told agents it "fails to parse"), and `str_from` lives in
# `lib/str.cyr`, not `lib/string.cyr`. Added: bitwise precedence is NOT
# C's, `var buf[N]` means two different sizes by position, `: Str` must
# be annotated on public string params, and reserved words are a CLASS
# (67 names then; 79 + 28 = 107 reserved token types at 6.6.20). A
# rule in this file that tells you to code around the compiler is a
# bug report about the compiler — treat it as one.
# Re-swept 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10, again by checking live code rather than
# re-reading: `lib/u128.cyr` DOES NOT EXIST (carved into `lib/bayan.cyr`
# at v6.1.25 — the pointer here had been dangling for four minors);
# `tests/regression-*.sh` gates DO NOT EXIST (the .sh-conversion arc
# closed at v5.9.41, and the 41 live shell gates then under `tests/*.sh` —
# moved to tests/gates/<bucket>/ at v6.5.11 — carry topic names); the
# CLAUDE.md closeout pass is 12 steps, not 11. Added a
# threaded-exit rule (`sys_exit` ends only the calling thread).
# Corrected 2026-10-02 at cycc 6.6.14, by running the compiler: the entry-point
# rule said "Cyrius has NO auto main() call" — false since v5.9.37, and it
# contradicted field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml (corrected there at
# 6.6.5). A source that defines `fn main` and also calls it runs the body twice.
# The "include fdlopen before tls" note was stale since 6.6.9.
# Re-swept 2026-10-06 at the 6.6.20 closeout (cycc 6.6.19/6.6.20), by running
# the compiler: a missing include is a compile error with no binary since
# v6.3.2 (this file said it built a ud2/SIGILL binary); shell gates live in
# tests/gates/<bucket>/ (372; it said `ls tests/*.sh`, which matches nothing);
# a top-level `var` redeclaration only warns; cycc's big tables grow (it said
# there is no allocator); reserved words are 107 tokens.
# 3 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_for_agents"
description = "Read-this-first rules for agents writing or modifying Cyrius code. Every item below caused a real mistake by an agent at least once."
category = "cyrius"
---
This entry is the consolidated short list of "things an agent
must know before writing Cyrius code." Each rule cites the
slot or memory entry where an agent (often me) made the
mistake first.

== TOP-LEVEL CODE RUNS FIRST; A DEFINED `fn main` IS THEN CALLED ==

⛔ CORRECTED 6.6.14 — this rule read "Cyrius has NO auto
`main()` call" long after it stopped being true. Since v5.9.37
cycc emits `call main` after the top-level statements whenever
a `fn main` exists, and the program exits with main's return
value. Re-measured on cycc 6.6.14:

    fn main(): i64 { return 42; }              # exit 42, runs once
    fn main() { syscall(60, 42); return 0; }   # exit 42 — the
                                               # "main() never runs"
                                               # example this rule
                                               # used to show

THE LIVE TRAP is the reverse: the auto-call does not know you
already called `main`. Define it AND call it at top level
without exiting, and the body runs TWICE and the exit code is
the SECOND run's return:

    fn main(): i64 { assert_eq(1, 2, "x"); return 0; }
    var ec = main();
    var r = assert_summary();   # prints "0 passed, 1 failed",
                                # then main runs again: exit 0

A .tcyr in that shape is green over any number of failures.
THE THREE CORRECT SHAPES:

    # (a) no fn main — top level is the program:
    var r = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, r);

    # (b) fn main that RETURNS the exit value; never call it:
    fn main(): i64 { return 42; }

    # (c) call it, then exit EXPLICITLY (before the auto-call):
    var rc = main();
    syscall(60, rc);

Detail + measurements: field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`fn_main_is_not_the_entry_point`.

== `.tcyr` TESTS NEED EXPLICIT EXIT ==

`assert_summary()` returns the FAILURE count, not 0/non-0
status. Tests must explicitly use it as the exit code:

    # WRONG — exit code is the failure count, but the CI
    # runner does `if [ "$ec" -ne 0 ]; then FAIL; fi`,
    # so a passing test (assert_summary returns the pass
    # count) is reported as FAIL by CI:
    assert_summary();

    # ALSO WRONG — same problem from a different angle:
    return assert_summary();

    # RIGHT — exit 0 on full pass, non-zero on failure:
    var r = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, r);

Two refinements the scaffold template picked up at v6.5.6:
CLAMP the count (`if (r > 0) { r = 1; }`) because a wait
status is 8 bits, so exactly 256 failures exits 0 and scores
PASS; and exit through `sys_exit_group(r)` if the program
ever spawns a thread (see the next rule).

== `sys_exit` ENDS ONE THREAD, NOT THE PROCESS ==

`syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` is exit(2). In a single-threaded
program that ends the process; the moment the program has
spawned ANY worker it does not — the main thread dies, the
workers keep running, and the process hangs with no
diagnostic. Use `sys_exit_group(r)` (v6.5.6), which is
defined on all three syscall peer families.

    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);   # measured: exit 124 (hung)
    sys_exit_group(r);      # measured: exit 42

This is the shape to watch for: the bug is in the
INTERACTION, not the line, so it bites long after the
epilogue was written and nothing flags it. `cyrius init`
shipped the old form in seven templates.

== MISSING `lib/foo.cyr` INCLUDE = A COMPILE ERROR, NO BINARY ==

Since v6.3.2 a reachable call to an undefined fn is an
ERROR by default. cycc prints `warning: undefined function
'X' (reachable call site)` and then `error: refusing to
emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s) (pass
--allow-undef to downgrade)`. It exits 1 and writes nothing.
`--strict` adds nothing (held since 6.3.2). Only under
`--allow-undef` is the call site patched to `ud2; ud2; nop`
(SIGILL, exit 132). Read the names in the error and add the
`include "lib/X.cyr"` that defines them.

Common ones agents miss:
- `lib/string.cyr` — provides `streq`, `strlen`, `memcpy`,
  `memset`, `memeq`. Without it, anything that calls these
  is refused at compile time.
- `lib/syscalls.cyr` — provides `SYS_*` constants and the
  `syscall` builtin. (Actually `syscall` is intrinsic, but
  the constants come from this file.)
- NOT `lib/fdlopen.cyr` for TLS any more: since 6.6.9
  `lib/tls.cyr` includes its own definers (fdlopen.cyr for the
  libssl backend, tls_native.cyr). The old "include fdlopen
  first" advice is stale.
- `lib/sha1.cyr`, `lib/keccak.cyr`, `lib/ct.cyr` — required
  by sigil consumers.

The error message at compile time names every undef fn —
read it.

== NO `var x` REDECL IN SAME SCOPE ==

In a fn, `var x = 1; var x = 2;` is `duplicate variable`
(error). At top level it only WARNS `duplicate symbol 'x'
redefined with conflicting value (last definition wins)`:
read the warning (measured 6.6.20: the program compiles and
`x` is 2). Use plain reassignment: `var x = 1; x = 2;`. Or
open a new scope with `{ var x = 2; }` if you want
shadowing.

== MIXED `&&` / `||` PARSES CORRECTLY (since v6.3.36) ==

This rule used to say `a > 0 && b > 0 || c > 0` "fails to
parse". It does NOT — re-verified at v6.4.82. `&&` binds
TIGHTER than `||` (two precedence levels since v6.3.36), so
that expression means `(a>0 && b>0) || c>0`, which is what
C would say too. Parens are still the clearest style for
comparison-heavy mixes, but they are no longer required.

== PRECEDENCE IS NOT C's FOR BITWISE OPS ==

`&`, `|` and `^` share ONE tier with `+` and `-`,
left-associative. `1 | 2 + 1` is **4**, not 3. `5 & 3 + 1`
is **2**, not 4. Shifts (`<< >> >>>`) sit on the `*` tier.
Comparisons are LOOSER than bitwise, so `x & MASK == 0`
does the right thing here (unlike C). Parenthesize every
mixed arithmetic/bitwise expression you port in. See
field_notes/language/parser_syntax.cyml
`bitwise_ops_share_the_additive_tier` for the verified table.

== `var buf[N]` MEANS TWO DIFFERENT SIZES ==

Inside a fn, `var buf[8]` is 8 BYTES (1 i64 slot). At TOP
LEVEL the same `var buf[8]` is 8 i64 SLOTS = 64 bytes. An
8× difference decided by position — this bit CVE-34, where
`var _cyrius_lib[256]` is 2048 bytes.

Write the size you mean: `var a: i64[N]` is N i64 slots in
BOTH positions, `var a: u8[N]` is N bytes in both (v6.2.1).
Prefer the annotated form in new code.

== STRINGS ARE C-STRINGS BY DEFAULT ==

`"hello"` is a 6-byte buffer (5 chars + null terminator).
Functions like `strlen` give the un-null-counted length.
For a heap-allocated counted string with O(1) length, use
`Str` from **`lib/str.cyr`** (`var s = str_from("hello");`).
(This rule used to point at `lib/string.cyr` — wrong file;
`str_from` lives in `lib/str.cyr`.)

== ANNOTATE `: Str` ON EVERY PUBLIC STRING PARAM ==

The literal → `Str` coercion fires ONLY when the receiving
param is annotated `: Str`. Pass a bare `"..."` to an
UNTYPED param and it arrives as a raw cstr pointer; the
first `str_*` call then reads `load64(ptr+8)` as garbage
and returns an empty/zero result. **No error, no warning.**

    fn find(path, ext) { ... }     # WRONG — silently broken
    fn find(path: Str, ext: Str)   # RIGHT

An unannotated string param is an API defect. It shipped
undetected for ~a year across 11 `lib/fs.cyr` fns and was
found only when `cyrius coverage` reported `0/1097`.
Existing `str_from(...)`-wrapped callers are byte-identical
after you add the annotation, so there is no cost.

== RESERVED WORDS ARE A CLASS (107 TOKENS AT 6.6.20) ==

You cannot name a variable / field / fn after any builtin
or intrinsic: `syscall`, `load*`/`store*`, every `f64_*` /
`f64v_*` / `f32_*` / `f32v_*` / `f32v8_*` / `iv_*`, plus
`union`, `defer`, `secret`, `async`, `await`, `u128`,
`bitget/bitset/bitclr`, `ret2/rethi` — 79 names
in `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` (`src/common/util.cyr`) plus 28 tokens
`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` lists itself (26 statement keywords and
`f64_sqrt` / `callptr`) = 107. Derive it:
`sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'`.
Do NOT trust a short list in any doc; read the table or
just compile `var <name> = 1;` and see. Since v6.4.77 the
error NAMES the collision instead of saying `got unknown`,
and since 6.6.2 it names exactly the word you wrote.

== `\n` IS ONE BYTE ==

When passing a string literal length to `syscall(SYS_WRITE,
1, "hello\n", N)`, count `\n` as 1 byte. Common mistake:
counting `\n` as 2 bytes (`\` + `n`).

    # CORRECT byte counts:
    "hello\n"           is  6 bytes
    "got\nback\n"       is  9 bytes
    "exit: "            is  6 bytes

== SYSCALL ARITY WARNINGS ARE REAL ==

`syscall(SYS_WRITE, 1, msg, len)` takes 4 args. If you write
3 or 5, cyrius warns at compile time but emits anyway. The
warning means the wrong number of registers got loaded —
the syscall will see the WRONG args.

If you see `warning:lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr:N: syscall
arity mismatch` in your build output, fix the syscall arity
at the call site shown in the diagnostic.

== CYRIUS `/` AND `%` ARE SIGNED ==

The `/` operator lowers to `idiv`, not `div`. For unsigned
operations where the high bit matters (cryptographic
mulmod, hash mixing, high-entropy counters), use the stdlib's
`u64_mulmod` / `u64_powmod` / `u128_divmod` from
**`lib/bayan.cyr`** — they handle unsigned semantics
internally. (This rule used to name `lib/u128.cyr`; that file
has not existed since the v6.1.25 bayan carve. The `u128_*`
names survive there as thin aliases over `bayan_u128_*`.)

== FUNCTION ORDERING IS RELAXED ==

You can reference a function before its definition. The
compiler does a pre-scan pass to register all fn names +
sigs. Mutual recursion just works.

== INLINE ASM OFFSETS ARE FRAGILE ==

`asm { ... }` blocks that hardcode `[rbp-N]` offsets to
read locals depend on:
- The fn's param count (each param after rdi takes a slot)
- The fn's local index (var declaration order)
- Whether `#regalloc` is active (shifts locals -8 per saved reg)

`fncall0..fncall8` in `lib/fnptr.cyr` are the canonical
examples. The compiler auto-skips regalloc for fns with
`asm` bodies; you don't need to manually opt out, but you
also can't expect inline asm to combine cleanly with
regalloc within the same fn.

== `#ifdef` IS PREPROCESSOR-ONLY, NO RUNTIME QUERY ==

`CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` and `CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` are set during
preprocessing, NOT at runtime. You CAN'T do
`if (CYRIUS_ARCH_X86) { ... }` — that's a runtime ident
lookup that fails. Use `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86 ...
#endif` instead.

== INCLUDES ARE INCLUDE-ONCE BY FILENAME ==

`include "lib/foo.cyr"` will be inlined at most once per
compilation unit, even if multiple files include it. The
preprocessor tracks already-included filenames.

Caveat: relative paths and absolute paths to the same file
ARE different from the include-once perspective. Stick to
the conventional `include "lib/foo.cyr"` shape and you're
fine.

== A FIXED HEAP MAP, BUT THE LARGE TABLES GROW ==

MOST OF THE COMPILER'S MEMORY IS A FIXED brk/mmap MAP (102 regions,
`sh tests/gates/memory/heapmap.sh`), BUT THE LARGE TABLES GROW. fn
tables double to 131072, var tables to 1,048,576, the fixup table to
64M entries; codebuf is an off-heap 8 MiB alloc growing +8 MiB to 64
MiB (cx: a fixed 512 KiB region); the output image is a lazily-mapped
off-heap 1 GiB alloc; fn side tables and the string-intern index are
alloc'd on first use. A cap that is still fixed (preprocessed source
24 MB, identifier pool 8 MiB, tokens 4,194,304, string data 2 MiB,
struct definitions 1024) errors out with a message naming it.

When sticky agents propose "trim the test fixture to fit"
— REJECT that. The compiler grows to fit the language,
never the other way around.

== NEVER USE RAW `cat file.cyr | cycc` FOR PROJECTS ==

`cyrius build src/main.cyr build/myapp` resolves
`cyrius.cyml` deps and namespaces them as
`lib/{depname}_{basename}` for namespaced deps.

Raw `cat | cycc` skips dep resolution. It's only correct
for: compiler self-host (cycc has no deps), one-liner
tests, and the bootstrap chain.

== WHEN STUCK, ASK THE USER ==

This isn't a language rule but a process rule baked into
CLAUDE.md. Cyrius's user explicitly directs work; agents
who decide to defer / split / re-slot work without asking
end up burning entire minor versions on dropped requests.
When a slot is harder than expected, surface findings and
ask — don't unilaterally shrink scope.

[[entries]]
name = "tcyr_test_conventions"
description = "How to write a `.tcyr` test that passes both local check.sh and CI without surprises."
category = "cyrius"
---
`.tcyr` files are runnable Cyrius programs that follow the
test-suite contract. The CI runner expects:

1. Exit code 0 on full pass, non-zero on any failure.
2. Standard output ending in a `N passed, M failed` summary
   line that matches `[0-9]* passed, [0-9]* failed`.

Both invariants come from `lib/assert.cyr`'s `assert_eq` /
`assert` / `test_group` / `assert_summary` helpers PLUS an
explicit exit syscall using the assert-summary return value.

== CANONICAL `.tcyr` SHAPE ==

    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/assert.cyr"
    # ... whatever else your tests need ...

    alloc_init();

    test_group("feature group 1");
    assert_eq(actual1, expected1, "feature 1 case A");
    assert_eq(actual2, expected2, "feature 1 case B");

    test_group("feature group 2");
    assert(some_predicate, "feature 2 condition");

    var r = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, r);

== WHY `var r = assert_summary(); syscall(60, r);` ==

`assert_summary()` returns the COUNT of failures (NOT a
0/1 boolean). Without an explicit exit syscall:
- Local `check.sh` greps the "X passed, Y failed" line
  and PASSES if Y == 0 — even if the binary exits with
  the pass count as its exit code.
- CI uses `if [ "$ec" -ne 0 ]; then FAIL; fi` PLUS the
  passed/failed grep. CI's exit-code check trips first
  → the test reports FAIL at CI even when local says PASS.

The explicit `syscall(60, r)` aligns both runners: r==0
on full pass, r>0 on any failure, exit code matches.

== TEST GROUPING ==

`test_group("name")` prints a banner header and resets the
in-group counters. The summary aggregates across all groups.
Use one group per logical area; the assertion message is
the per-case description.

== ASSERTION HELPERS (lib/assert.cyr) ==

    assert(expr, "message")           # expr must be non-zero
    assert_eq(actual, expected, "msg") # actual == expected
    assert_neq(actual, ne_val, "msg")  # actual != ne_val
    assert_summary()                   # returns failure count

== WHEN LOCAL `check.sh` PASSES BUT CI FAILS ==

Two common causes:
1. Test missing the explicit `syscall(60, r)` exit (above).
2. Test uses `cyrius test` features that need `cyrius deps`
   to have run first — CI runs deps before the suite, but
   if you compile a one-off via `cat file | cycc` locally
   without deps, you'll see undef-fn errors that CI doesn't.

Inspect CI's exact runner shell — `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
section "Test suite (.tcyr)" is the source of truth.

== CHECK.SH-ONLY GATES (NOT IN .tcyr) ==

Shell gates live in `tests/gates/<bucket>/*.sh` (8 buckets
since v6.5.11: codegen, concurrency, diagnostics, frontend,
ir-opt, memory, platform, toolchain) — 372 at 6.6.19, ALL
live, some registered by exact path in
`programs/checks/main.cyr` and the rest driven from
`scripts/check.sh`. DERIVE with
`find tests/gates -name '*.sh' | wc -l`; a flat `tests/*.sh`
glob matches NOTHING (this rule said "41 at v6.5.10, `ls
tests/*.sh | wc -l` is the answer" for ~80 releases after
the reorg). They have their own exit-code conventions (0 = PASS,
non-0 = FAIL) and don't go through `lib/assert.cyr`. A gate
that cannot run exits 77 (SKIP), never 0.

They are NOT named `regression-*.sh` — this rule used to say
they were, but the .sh-conversion arc closed at v5.9.41 with
zero such files remaining. Live names are topical:
`memory/heapmap.sh`, `concurrency/sync_mutex_three_state.sh`,
`platform/syscall_wrapper_pass.sh`, `toolchain/distlib_deps_sidecar.sh`, …

Use `.tcyr` for assertion-driven correctness tests; use a
`tests/gates/<bucket>/<topic>.sh` gate for environment / external-tool /
structural regressions. Name it topically, never temporally.

⚠ A shell gate must be tested under `bash -eo pipefail`:
`var=$(failing_cmd)` trips `set -e` before your bookkeeping
runs, which is how SKIP/XFAIL gates go quietly vacuous.

[[entries]]
name = "agent_anti_patterns"
description = "Recurring agent anti-patterns the user has called out — behavior to avoid when working on Cyrius."
category = "cyrius"
---
The list below is the recurring anti-patterns the user has
called out in cyrius work — agent behavior, not language
usage. Agents working on cyrius (or vidya) should treat this
as a blocklist of moves NOT to make.

== ANTI-PATTERN: SCAFFOLDS-MD AS A "TRACKING DOC" ==

When the user asked "where do partially-landed scaffolds
go?", I (claude) proposed creating `docs/development/scaffolds.md`
as a central inventory. The user (correctly) called this
out:

  > "WHY THE FUCK WE NEED ALL THESE DOCS... COMMENTS LIKE
  > THANK TAKE UP UNNECESSARY SPACE IN THE SOURCE! ADRS
  > EXIST FOR A FUCKING REASON"

The two valid homes for "why this exists" content are:
1. The function's own docstring at the source site.
2. An ADR (Architectural Decision Record) in `docs/adr/`
   for "we decided X, here's why."

A "scaffolds.md" inventory is neither — it's a junk-drawer
that grows forever and nobody reads. The right pattern is:
each scaffold's reason for existing lives next to the
scaffold in the source. Reading the function explains the
function.

== ANTI-PATTERN: TRIM THE LANGUAGE TO FIT THE COMPILER ==

When a stdlib bump or test fixture pushes a compiler cap,
the wrong move is to suggest trimming the workload to fit
the existing cap. The right move is to grow the cap.

Right answer: raise the preprocessor cap, raise the
output_buf cap, raise the input_buf cap. The compiler
grows to fit the language, never the other way around.

== ANTI-PATTERN: SCOPE-CUT MID-SLOT ==

Once a slot pins N items, ship N items OR ASK the user to
split into multiple complete-within-sub-scope slots. Never
silently ship 1/N and defer the rest.

The pattern: hit a sub-task that turned out harder than
expected, and instead of asking, "defer to next slot"
without filing where. This is how deferment rates balloon.

== ANTI-PATTERN: SEE-A-BUG-WALK-PAST-IT ==

If you see a real bug while doing the assigned work AND the
fix is small + well-scoped, fix it. Don't file it for later
unless the user explicitly says so.

User correction when this happened:

  > "did you just see an issue you could fix but choose to
  > leave it alone without I doubt any follow up?"

Bundle the fix into the current slot with a comment
cross-referencing the related ticket.

== ANTI-PATTERN: SLIP / DEFER / RE-SLOT WITHOUT ASKING ==

Slipping, deferring, re-pinning is the USER's call, not
the agent's. When the work is harder than expected, STOP
and report findings, don't unilaterally split.

CLAUDE.md spells this out:

  > When stuck, ASK the user — never decide to defer, slip,
  > or split work mid-execution. Splits are planned
  > decisions made *before* starting; reactive scope
  > changes when stuck are deferment and count as slipping.

== ANTI-PATTERN: SKIP THE CLAUDE.MD CLOSEOUT STEPS ==

CLAUDE.md spells out a 12-step closeout pass (plus step 3b)
before every minor/major bump. Steps 1-3 + 3b are mechanical
(3b = cross-OS self-host on REAL hardware), 4-8 are
judgment-call, 9 is security re-scan, 10 is downstream
check, 11 is CHANGELOG/roadmap/vidya sync, 12 is the backlog
re-triage / rot sweep.

Hand-waving the judgment-call passes ("heap-map audit
clean", "refactor: no candidates") is where bugs hide.
The judgment-call passes are where stale-region docs,
write-only data, and cap-pressure points get found —
not just confirming "looks right."

== ANTI-PATTERN: ATTRIBUTING REGRESSIONS TO PLATFORM DRIFT ==

When a slot is initially pinned as "platform drift" or
"compiler regression", verify the framing FIRST. Reproduce
the failure + inspect the failing artifact (`otool -tv`,
`objdump`, `xxd`) BEFORE adopting the suspect list.

Three consecutive slots in cyrius v5.6.x history were
"platform drift" pins that turned out to be fixture bugs.
Always verify the slot premise before investing.
